Twist TODO List
================

SCP/SFTP (src/ssh, over foxtrot)

  - A transfer that fails, even for a missing file, closes its connection, so
    the next URL logs in again.  An SFTP status that leaves the channel sound
    could keep it.
  - A parked connection that the server closed an instant before it is taken
    fails the transfer with "closed" and does not retry on a new one.  The
    command line runs the loop once before each URL, which catches closes that
    have already arrived; the window left is a race.
  - Nothing parked is sent a disconnect when it is dropped (idle for two
    minutes, or at exit); the TCP connection just closes.
  - There is no way to forbid reuse, in the library or in the tool.
  - Ask foxtrot for the number of requests outstanding in an SFTP client.
    gf_sftp_client_pending is the bytes queued, so twist counts its own (sftp.c
    sent () and inflight).
  - Proxies: socks5:// is treated as socks5h:// (the name goes to the proxy to
    look up; socks5:// should look it up itself).  socks4:// proxies are
    refused.  Credentials are sent for tunnels only: a plain http:// URL
    through an HTTP proxy gets no Proxy-Authorization.
  - statvfs, and the other commands the sftp tool has, as -Q commands;
    --create-file-mode and the directory mode.
  - Quote commands for scp URLs.
  - An HTTP/3 URL does not take a zone: quic_connect reads the host with
    inet_pton itself.  Only the TCP dialers pass the scope.
  - --limit-rate for the other protocols: the pacer is in src/engine/rate.c,
    and only the SFTP pumps use it.  Until then the command line refuses the
    option for other URLs.
  - Certificates are found only as KEY-cert.pub beside a key file; there is no
    option to name a certificate elsewhere (ssh's CertificateFile).
  - -l for ftp URLs, which would send NLST; it only affects sftp now.
  - -C and -T completion of the URL for the schemes other than sftp and scp.
  - Keyboard-interactive questions that need a person, such as a one-time
    code: twist answers only a single hidden question with the password.  A
    prompt on the terminal would need a way for the application to be asked.
  - Passphrase-protected OpenSSH-format private keys.  Blocked on bcrypt-pbkdf
    being available in libgcrypt; it is deliberately not taken from anywhere
    else.  An ssh-agent holding the key is the workaround, and works.


FTPS (src/ftp/ftp.c)

  - Session reuse needs a TLS 1.3 ticket from the control connection.  Against
    a TLS 1.2 server, or one that gave none, the data connection does a full
    handshake, and a server that requires reuse then refuses it.
  - A data connection that ends without a close_notify is taken as the end of
    the file, as it is for ftp, so a truncated transfer is not noticed (the
    size in the 150 reply is parsed and not used for this).
  - No CCC, and no PROT C or PROT S choice: PROT P or nothing.
  - ftps through an http:// or socks5:// proxy, which runs TLS on the tunnel,
    has not been run (integration/ftp-proxy.sh does plain ftp).


HTTP

  - Cookies (src/http/cookie.c): Expires and Max-Age are not read, so a cookie
    lives as long as the jar and Max-Age=0 does not delete.  SameSite and
    HttpOnly are not looked at.
  - The output buffer of a connection has no limit.  A handler that queues
    without end against a peer that does not read grows it, so each handler
    limits itself (SFTP does, by its window); none does for a body given in
    one piece, which the application chose the size of.
  - --connect-timeout for ldap covers the lookup and the TCP connect only; a
    server that takes the connection and does not answer the bind is waited for
    until --max-time.  What curl does for ldap is not known: the curl it was
    compared with has no LDAP.
  - URL globbing does not reach the file given to -T ({a,b} there, with #N in
    the URL), nor #N in --output-dir.  -T takes the name as written.
  - STARTTLS for ldap (curl has it through OpenLDAP only).  A user name and
    password for ldap without the s go in the clear.


https proxies (src/engine/tunnel.c, masque.c)

  - CONNECT-UDP (RFC 9298) has no end-to-end test: the only proxy to test
    against was a Go program, which has been removed.  The unit tests
    (tests/test-masque.c) remain.  HTTP/3 directly has none either.
  - HTTP/3 under a proxy is chosen only by --http3-only (the target's HTTPS
    record and Alt-Svc are not asked for, since the proxy does the lookup).
  - DNS over QUIC does not use a proxy.
  - The tunnel's packets are at most 1200 bytes (twist asks for that with
    max_udp_payload_size, or a peer's path MTU probes could exceed what the
    proxy's own datagrams hold, and some proxies then end the flow), so a
    larger path MTU is not used.
  - Templated CONNECT-TCP (draft-ietf-httpbis-connect-tcp), and
    --proxy-template-tcp for it, when it is an RFC.
  - ftp and ftps through an https proxy (the data connection too).
  - An http:// URL is tunnelled (CONNECT) and not sent in the proxy's absolute
    form, which some proxies would rather have.
  - A tunnel has an HTTP/2 connection of its own; one connection could carry
    many.
  - quickstep's gq_tls_free does not free the selected transcript (t->tr), so
    each TLS connection leaks about 1.3 KB.


Progress meter (see HACKING)

  - An upload is counted when the body is handed to the connection (HTTP,
    SMTP, MQTT, TFTP), not when the socket has taken it; sftp counts when the
    server has acknowledged a write.  A large POST on a slow link is at 100%
    sooner than it should be.
  - No size is known for POP3, LDAP, DICT, Gopher, WebSocket, MQTT or a
    directory listing.  For FTP the size is the number the reply to RETR may
    carry; asking the server (SIZE) would give it more often.
  - An HTTP response that is coded (--compressed) is counted by its bytes on
    the wire, which is what Content-Length counts; TWISTINFO_SIZE_DOWNLOAD is
    the decoded.
  - sftp and scp ask for the size of the file (one more round trip) only when
    the meter is on.


DNS (src/dns, RFC 8305)

  - Happy Eyeballs for the connection.  The resolver asks for AAAA, A and HTTPS
    together and gives the addresses through twist_dns_resolve_sink, IPv6 first
    and the families taking turns, as they come (with the 50 ms resolution
    delay).  No handler uses that yet: they take one address (IPv4 if there is
    one, else IPv6), so a name with both is reached over IPv4 and a broken IPv4
    path is not worked around.  What is wanted is a dialer that tries the next
    address after 250 ms, or at once when an attempt fails, and keeps the first
    to connect (a race between sockets that hands the winner's to the
    connection), and the HTTP, mail-link and FTP code moved onto it.
  - The hosts file gives the first IPv4 and the first IPv6 address of a name,
    and localhost is 127.0.0.1 only; both should give both families, ::1 too.
  - The cache holds one address for a name and is not used with the sink.
  - DoQ and DoH send their queries one after another (three round trips for an
    http URL); DoT, TCP and UDP send them together.  DoH can only be pipelined
    over HTTP/2, and DoQ needs a stream for each, since every id is 0.
  - RFC 10029: the types in one message, the extra ones in an MQTYPE-Query
    option, and the server says which it included.  None of 1.1.1.1, 8.8.8.8
    and 9.9.9.9 does yet (tried), so the queries go out separately in one
    flight.  gumdrop has the codec (dns/DnsMultiQType.java); a server that
    answers with MQTYPE-Response could be remembered and sent one message.
  - A UDP query is not sent again when it is lost: it is waited for until the
    transfer's timeout.
  - With the default order a discovery query (DDR) is made before every lookup
    that is not in the cache, and its outcome is not remembered.
  - What has not been answered 2 s after the first address is given up on (the
    lookup is complete then): a very slow nameserver loses its other types.

Environment

  - A configuration file.
  - A script that sets CURL_CA_BUNDLE gets the system's authorities; whether
    to read it is undecided.
  - SSL_CERT_DIR is read as a directory of PEM files by name (.pem, .crt, .cer
    or a c_rehash name), not through OpenSSL's rules.


Tests

  - HTTP/3 closes the connection when the server opens a bidirectional stream
    (twist_quic_stream_action, tested), but the callback that does it
    (ev_stream_data in quic.c) is not run by any test: it needs a QUIC peer
    that opens one, and there is none in the tree.
  - Discovered resolvers (RFC 9462).  A DoT or DoQ resolver found with a
    _dns.resolver.arpa query must have a certificate valid for the address that
    was asked as well as for its own name (twist_verify_chain_names, tested
    with a certificate in tests/test-tls.c).  The wiring from the resolver
    through twist_connection_require_name and the quic option has no test that
    runs it: the nameserver is read from /etc/resolv.conf, so a fake one cannot
    be put in front of twist without root.
  - Fuzzing covers the parsers in the library (see HACKING).  Not reached: the
    command line's own parsing (main.c: options, the speed and time arguments,
    --write-out), which is static there; the DNS cache and resolver state
    machines; the SFTP engine's use of foxtrot, which could be driven by a
    fuzzed server session; the Set-Cookie/HSTS persistence; and the file
    scheme.  A fuzzer of a whole transfer, with a scripted peer on a
    socketpair, would reach the connection and handler glue.
  - Decide how integration peers are provided.  make check stays in-process.
    make check-integration dials a peer and skips with exit 77 when the peer is
    absent.  A podman lab is one candidate and is not chosen yet.


Runtime

  - The storage pool has two threads, shared by the file handler's reads and
    the SSH key and known_hosts loads.  Letting go of a file transfer no longer
    waits for its worker, but a read that never comes back still holds one of
    the two until the process ends, and two such reads hold up every file
    transfer and SSH load behind them.  A larger pool only moves the limit; a
    read that can be given up on would need a thread to be cancelled, or a
    different way to read.
  - An io_uring poll backend.


Protocols not yet done

  - SMB/SMBS.
  - RTMP/RTMPS.
  - NTLM and Negotiate, only after an explicit dependency decision.
